<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Taebong</h1><p class="st-meta">901 CE – 918 CE · capital Cheorwon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the one-eyed monk-king Gung Ye in central Korea, from whose collapse Wang Geon founded Goryeo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a>, said to be an outcast <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> prince who lost an eye as an infant, became a monk and then a rebel commander in the 890s. Gathering support in the central and northern regions, including from the Wang family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Songak</a>, he proclaimed a kingdom in 901 called Later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a>, appealing to the memory of Goguryeo.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state changed name to Majin in 904 and to Taebong in 911, and moved its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Songak</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheorwon">Cheorwon</a> in 905. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a> set up a central bureaucracy with new ministries and expanded southward, using the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> to win battles against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a>. He increasingly proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya Buddha</a> and ruled with suspicion and violence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 918 leading generals overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a>, who was killed while fleeing, and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> on the throne. Wang Geon renamed the state <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a> and moved the capital back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Songak</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taebong.jpg" alt="Taebong" width="320" height="378"><figcaption>Image: KJS615 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:History_of_Korea-Later_three_Kingdoms_Period-915_CE.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>901 CE – 918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cheorwon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gung Ye</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Goguryeo, Hugoguryeo, Majin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=taebong" aria-label="Taebong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7834 813 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taebong lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8154 1069L8260 1139M8154 1069L8154 1069"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8260 1139h0M8154 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8154 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8154 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=taebong">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
